\end{align*}

Now, let's calculate the value of each term modulo 7:

\begin{align*}
7k + 4 & \equiv 4 \pmod{7} \\
7k + 6 & \equiv 6 \pmod{7} \\
7k + 8 & \equiv 1 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}

Now, we can substitute these values back into the expression:

$$(n + 2)(n + 4)(n + 6) \equiv (4)(6)(1) \pmod{7}$$

Now, we can calculate the product of these values modulo 7:

\begin{align*}
(4)(6)(1) & = 24 \\
& \equiv 3 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the remainder when $(n + 2)(n + 4)(n + 6)$ is divided by 7 is $\boxed{3}$.

