To find out how much Susan pays for the tickets, we first need to find out the discount she gets on each ticket, then multiply that by the number of tickets. 

The discount she gets on each ticket is 25% of $\$20$, which is:

\begin{align*}
\text{Discount} &= \text{price of ticket} \times \text{discount percentage} \\
&= \$20 \times 0.25 \\
&= \$5
\end{align*}

Now, we find out the price she pays per ticket. 

\begin{align*}
\text{Price paid per ticket} &= \text{price of ticket} - \text{discount} \\
&= \$20 - \$5 \\
&= \$15
\end{align*}

Since she buys 4 tickets, the total amount Susan pays is:

\begin{align*}
\text{Total amount paid by Susan} &= \text{price paid per ticket} \times \text{number of tickets} \\
&= \$15 \times 4 \\
&= \$60
\end{align*}

To find out the discount Pam gets on each ticket, we first find 30% of $\$20$, which is:

\begin{align*}
\text{Discount} &= \text{price of ticket} \times \text{discount percentage} \\
&= \$20 \times 0.3 \\
&= \$6
\end{align*}

Now, we find the price she pays per ticket.

\begin{align*}
\text{Price paid per ticket} &= \text{price of ticket} - \text{discount} \\
&= \$20 - \$6 \\
&= \$14
\end{align*}

Since she buys 5 tickets, the total amount Pam pays is:

\begin{align*}
\text{Total amount paid by Pam} &= \text{price paid per ticket} \times \text{number of tickets} \\
&= \$14 \times 5 \\
&= \$70
\end{align*}

Now we find the difference between the total amount paid by Pam and the total amount paid by Susan:

\begin{align*}
\text{Difference} &= \text{total amount paid by Pam} - \text{total amount paid by Susan} \\
&= \$70 - \$60 \\
&= \$10
\end{align*}

So the final answer is:

\boxed{\$10}

